153. Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array
Suppose an array of length n sorted in ascending order is rotated between 1 and n times. 
For example, the array nums = [0,1,2,4,5,6,7] might become:
[4,5,6,7,0,1,2] if it was rotated 4 times.
[0,1,2,4,5,6,7] if it was rotated 7 times.
Notice that rotating an array [a[0], a[1], a[2], ..., a[n-1]] 1 time results in the array [a[n-1], a[0], a[1], a[2], ..., a[n-2]].
Given the sorted rotated array nums of unique elements, return the minimum element of this array.
You must write an algorithm that runs in O(log n) time.

Explanation:

🧠 Core Mental Model (THIS is the logic)
Key Truth:
  In a rotated sorted array with distinct elements:
  At any time, at least one half of the array is fully sorted.

Your Way of Thinking (High-Level)
You are doing three things repeatedly:
  1️⃣ Identify which half is sorted
      You check: nums[left] <= nums[mid]
      If this is true → left half is sorted
      Else → right half is sorted

  2️⃣ Extract guaranteed information from the sorted half

      Here is the crucial idea: The minimum of a sorted half is its first element.
      So: 
        If left half is sorted → minimum is nums[left]
        If right half is sorted → minimum is nums[mid]
        You safely compare it with your current min.

  3️⃣ Discard the sorted half

      Why can we throw it away?
      Because:
          You already considered its minimum
          If a smaller value exists, it must be in the unsorted half
          So you move pointers to search only the unsorted part.

          This is why the search space keeps shrinking.

🧩 Visual Intuition
Example:

[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]
 L       M   R

Left half is sorted → min is 4
But smaller values may exist → search right half
[0,1,2]
Again sorted → min is 0 → done

🔁 Loop Invariant (VERY IMPORTANT)  
  “At every iteration, min stores the smallest value seen so far,
  and the remaining search space still may contain the true minimum.”

  This invariant is never violated.

Code:

class Solution {
    public int findMin(int[] nums) {
        int n=nums.length;
        int left=0; int right=n-1;

        int min=nums[0];
        
        while(left<=right){
            int mid=(left+right)/2;
            if(nums[left]<=nums[mid]){
                min=Math.min(min,nums[left]);
                left=mid+1;
            }
            else{
                min=Math.min(min,nums[mid]);
                right=mid-1;
            }
        }
        return min;
    }
}
